 a period of about 55  minutes so you can imagine that this is  several several fold orders faster in  magnitude than if you would do a  sequencing reaction we also came up with  ideas to figure out what would be the  right concentrations where to put the  biotin labels in these  amplicons and then we can see that up  till the ct value of close to 35 we can  specifically distinguish between a  substrate which is um uh  positive and also alpha variant negative  versus the substrate which is both  positive for infection as well as is a  variant of concern  because of the presence of the mutation  interestingly this particular technology  of variant detection can actually be  extended to cover the majority of  variance of concern because like i said  the mutations ah that need that are  detected and the presence of the  mismatches where it should be there on  the guide rna can actually is is quite  unifor universal so this means that the  mutation of signal signatures of all the  variants of concern  can be captured using this ray platform  and the s in cas9 due to the way that it  recognizes the dna can give the two  different types of signal where one it  is bound to the substrate strongly and  in the other case it is evicted from the  substrate  thus this is a platform that is not only  very useful for uh the variant of  concern for start forward diagnostics  but it is also useful if one wants to  use it for any kind of other single  nucleotide variants such as those found  in diseases or those associated with  certain types of traits um or those  which are just present in the dna and  one needs to detect them for a  particular asset  these kind of assays can help in  multiple ways one of the one of the ways  that it can  help is for early detection of variance  and this is something that we had  you know we can we had  [Music]  put forward in a landsat commentary  this year  where the variant diagnosis which  normally takes place by high support  sequencing can actually be streamlined  by having a initial cutoff based on a  rapid diagnostic platform such as  through crispr one can detect these  variant signatures and then can very  nicely  decide on the fate of such individuals  whether they need to be quarantined in a  stronger way or they need to be  quarantined in a normal of you know home  quarantine or they can be released  because certain variants are more  susceptible for transmission as has been  shown earlier than the other so these  are the type of streaming approaches  that one might potentially uh benefit  from if one has a diagnostic assays  which are so specific for detecting this  point mutation  so with that i come actually uh to the  most important slide which is the  acknowledgement slide and there are a  large number of people who have been  working on this  for a long period of time almost one and  a half years now uh during the entire  pandemic these are students post-doc  scientists  lab technicians  and  we are a big team who have been working  on this for a long period of time  actually  and we have been generally funded very  generously by different funding agencies  both government and non-government  and i'm extremely glad to get an  opportunity today to present this before  you  and would be happy  to address questions if you might have  based on this thank you very much


